# Abstract

A guided surface waveguide probe is disclosed for launching a guided surface wave along the surface of a lossy conducting medium, such as the Earth. The probe comprises a charge terminal elevated above the lossy conducting medium and a feed network configured to excite the charge terminal with a phase delay. The charge terminal exhibits a self-capacitance, and the feed network further comprises a variable inductance or variable capacitance element coupled between the charge terminal and an excitation source, such that a total effective height of the probe can be adjusted. The phase delay imparted by the feed network, in combination with the physical height and effective capacitance of the charge terminal, is configured such that a wave tilt of a resulting electric field at a Hankel crossover distance substantially matches a wave tilt angle associated with a complex Brewster angle of incidence for the lossy conducting medium. By aligning the phase delay and effective height with the complex Brewster angle, the guided surface waveguide probe substantially mode-matches to a guided surface waveguide mode supported by the lossy conducting medium, thereby launching a guided surface wave that propagates with minimal radiation loss along the surface of the lossy conducting medium, rather than as a radiated wave that propagates into space.

---

# Method Claim

**Claim 1.** A method for launching a guided surface wave along a surface of a lossy conducting medium, the method comprising:

providing a charge terminal positioned at a physical height above the lossy conducting medium, the charge terminal having a self-capacitance;

determining a complex Brewster angle of incidence associated with the lossy conducting medium based at least in part on a permittivity and a conductivity of the lossy conducting medium at an operating frequency;

determining a wave tilt angle associated with the complex Brewster angle of incidence;

adjusting an effective height of the charge terminal by varying at least one of a variable inductance or a variable capacitance coupled between the charge terminal and a feed network, such that the effective height corresponds to the physical height combined with a phase delay associated with propagation of a charge terminal voltage;

exciting the charge terminal via the feed network with an excitation voltage phase-delayed relative to a source signal, the phase delay being selected in conjunction with the effective height such that a wave tilt of an electric field produced by the charge terminal at a Hankel crossover distance from the charge terminal substantially matches the wave tilt angle associated with the complex Brewster angle of incidence; and

responsive to the exciting, launching a guided surface wave that propagates along the surface of the lossy conducting medium in a guided surface waveguide mode substantially mode-matched to the lossy conducting medium, wherein the guided surface wave exhibits a field strength that decays substantially as an inverse of distance from the charge terminal along the surface.
